      "writtenOn": "2026-02-23T11:04:32Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Looking again, I believe the current design is better, it enforces the ordering between `pkvm_pviommu_finalise` and `kvm_guest_prepare_stage2`.",
      "parentUuid": "92eb02cd_82771287",
      "revId": "5245322e24a8e9787e55fa98295828034d9c83c4",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e3323fc3_7a9f9172",
        "filename": "arch/arm64/kvm/hyp/nvhe/mem_protect.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 352,
      "author": {
        "id": 1951687
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-02-23T11:18:04Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It is just \"has_devices\" sounds very specific and I wouldn\u0027t want to reproduce the memory donation nightware where we have bool all over the place to say \"is_memory\" \"update_mmio\", \"check_refcount\" ... That makes the calls unreadable and we have the need to \"transport\" this information that could be found somewhere else.\n\nAlthough you are completely right that it forces a dependency between pviommu and guest_prepare... You could also just INIT_LIST() in pkvm_pviommu_finalise() and then pkvm_vm_has_devices() can just WARN_ON(hyp_vm.next);\n\nAlternatively, how about kvm_guest_prepare_stage2() takes the stage-2 flags as an argument? And then the caller can set the right one depending on if devices where found or not? And then a pkvm_vm_has_devices() would even be safe as we know it is called after pviommu_finalise?",
      "parentUuid": "98e9a6b0_86c28669",
      "revId": "5245322e24a8e9787e55fa98295828034d9c83c4",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "69610040_b8eda241",
        "filename": "arch/arm64/kvm/hyp/nvhe/mem_protect.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 352,
      "author": {
        "id": 1951687
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-02-23T11:33:13Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I meant WARN_ON(!hyp_vm.next); (it works because INIT_LIST_HEAD() sets .next and .prev to itself)\n\nGiving more thoughts I wonder if just passing \"flags\" isn\u0027t just better as it doesn\u0027t create any dependency between mem_protect and pviommu.c pkvm.c is just doing the junction between both.",
      "parentUuid": "e3323fc3_7a9f9172",
      "revId": "5245322e24a8e9787e55fa98295828034d9c83c4",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "48e9b2b0_ffdb5d21",
        "filename": "arch/arm64/kvm/hyp/nvhe/mem_protect.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 352,
      "author": {
        "id": 1965911
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-02-23T12:18:33Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t like having `WARN_ON` when not necessarily, that also proved to be painful in the IOMMU code.\nPassing flags would be better, adding that in pkvm.c doesn\u0027t sound 2 bad.\n\nMy idea behind `has_devices` is that the VM might have some special handling for VMs with devices in the future beyond FWB, but that can always change as it is not an ABI.",
      "parentUuid": "69610040_b8eda241",
      "revId": "5245322e24a8e9787e55fa98295828034d9c83c4",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
